%C - This is a utility inspect or manipulate GPIOs

 
Syntax:
    gpio  <options>    

Options (to override autodetected defaults):

    m          GPIO Module [0:7]
    n          GPIO number with module [0:127]
    
    a          Set GPIO as input, and attach an interrupt
    c<"command">    Command line, command to run when interrupt is raised
    d           Dump GPIO
    h           Set GPIO to output high
    l           Set GPIO to output low
    i           Set GPIO to input
    o           Set GPIO to output
    
Examples:
    # Set GPIO0_0 to input, and detect on rising edge.  Attach IRQ 392
    # and run 'date' each time interrupt is hit.  
    gpio -m0 -n0 -a392 -i -r1 -c"date" &
    
    # Dump out current settings for GPIO1_2
    gpio -m1 -n2 -d
    
Notes:
    - Error / range checking of values for validity is not implemented in this utility.
    
    - The CTRLMMR_PADCONFx register is not set as part of this utility.  A recommended value is 0x40007, 
      where the '7' can change depending on the how the modes of the pin are defined, see Data Manaul for
      pin defintions.
    
         For Example: gpio0_0 uses register CTRMMR_PADCONFIG0 (0x0011C000)
             To read current value: in32 0x0011c0000
             To write current value : out32 0x0011c000 0x40007

    - This example application has been written and tested for Main domain GPIOs only.
    
J721EVM: 
         If testing on the J721e EVM, there is a push button (SW10) available that can be used to drive
         GPIO0_0.  As per above examples and notes, the interrupt for push button (SW10) can be caught
         by using the below command line, commands:
         
         # Set PADCONFIG for GPIO0_0
         out32 0x0011c000 0x40007
         
         # Configure GPIO module, Interrupt Mux, and attach interrupt
         gpio-j7 -m0 -n0 -a392 -i -r1 &
         
         # To see bit fields being set / unset, when pushing / holding SW10
         gpio -m0 -n0 -d
